.\" pwt - Power Worktrees manual page
.\" Version and date are updated by scripts/release.sh
.TH PWT 1 "October 2026" "pwt 0.2.18" "User Commands"

.SH NAME
pwt \- Power Worktrees: Git worktree manager for multiple projects

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pwt
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fICOMMAND\fR [\fIARGS\fR]
.br
.B pwt
\fIPROJECT\fR \fICOMMAND\fR [\fIARGS\fR]
.br
.B pwt
\fIPROJECT\fR \fIWORKTREE\fR \fICOMMAND\fR [\fIARGS\fR]

.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pwt
(Power Worktrees) is a comprehensive tool for managing Git worktrees across multiple projects. It provides isolated development environments with automatic port allocation, custom commands via Pwtfile, and seamless navigation between worktrees.
.PP
When the first argument is both a builtin command and a project name, the
command wins and a warning on stderr names the project's own routes: its
alias, or \fB\-\-project\fR \fINAME\fR. Worktrees follow the same
precedence.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-\-project " " \fINAME\fR
Specify project explicitly (useful when running from outside a project).
Alone, with no command, navigates to the project's main checkout.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show help message
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-V ", " \-\-version
Show version information

.SH COMMANDS

.SS Project Management
.TP
.B init \fR[\fIURL\fR]
Initialize a project. Without URL, configures current directory. With URL, clones and configures.
.TP
.B project list
List all configured projects
.TP
.B project show \fINAME\fR
Show project configuration
.TP
.B project set \fINAME\fR \fIKEY\fR \fIVALUE\fR
Update project configuration

.SS Worktree Operations
.TP
.B create\fR|\fBadd \fIBRANCH\fR [\fIBASE\fR] [\fI"DESCRIPTION"\fR]
Create a new worktree. Allocates port automatically and optionally creates branch.
Quoted text with spaces is auto-detected as description (not a branch name).
.RS
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Show what would be created without creating
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-editor
Open editor after creating
.TP
.BR \-\-from " " \fIREF\fR
Create from specific ref (tag, commit, branch)
.TP
.BR \-\-from\-current
Create from current branch
.TP
.BR \-\-branch " " \fINAME\fR
Use exact Git branch name while keeping the worktree name separate
.TP
.BR \-\-track " " \fIREMOTE\-BRANCH\fR
Create a local branch tracking an existing remote branch without applying branch_prefix
.TP
.BR \-\-detach
Check out \fIBASE\fR (a commit, tag or branch) detached: no branch is
created and \fIBRANCH\fR only names the worktree. A ref this clone lacks is
fetched by name. Cannot be combined with \fB\-\-branch\fR, \fB\-\-track\fR
or \fB\-\-clone\fR.
.TP
.BR \-\-no\-use
Do not make the new worktree current (default: switch to it).
.RE
.TP
.B repoint \fINAME\fR \fIREF\fR [\fB\-\-stop\fR] [\fB\-\-progress json\fR]
Check out \fIREF\fR detached in an existing worktree, keeping its port,
metadata, databases and caches; only the files that differ are rewritten.
Then runs the Pwtfile's \fBrepoint()\fR with \fB$PWT_CHANGED_FILES\fR and
\fB$PWT_PREVIOUS_COMMIT\fR. Refuses (exit 4) while a pwt job runs for the
worktree (\fB\-\-stop\fR stops them first), while something listens on its
port, or with uncommitted changes to tracked files. Exits 3 for an unknown
worktree or ref, 1 when \fBrepoint()\fR fails. Progress is written to
\fI~/.pwt/state/PROJECT/NAME.repoint.jsonl\fR; \fB\-\-progress json\fR
streams it on stdout.
.TP
.B track \fIREMOTE\-BRANCH\fR [\fB\-\-name\fR \fINAME\fR]
Create a pwt-managed worktree that tracks an existing remote branch. The local
branch matches the remote branch name without applying branch_prefix. If
\fB\-\-name\fR is omitted, pwt uses a ticket-like token such as PROJ-1234 when
present, otherwise the last branch path segment.
.TP
.B adopt \fR[\fIPATH\fR]
Register an existing git worktree with pwt, allocate metadata/port, and run
standard setup hooks. Defaults to the current directory. Adopted worktrees may
live outside the configured worktrees_dir; pwt records their real path and shows
them in list, cd, use, current, and info commands.
.TP
.B setup \fR[\fIPATH\fR]
Alias for adopt.
.TP
.B remove\fR|\fBrm \fINAME\fR
Remove a worktree and clean up resources
.RS
.TP
.BR \-\-with\-branch
Also delete the branch (if merged)
.TP
.BR \-\-force\-branch
Force delete the branch (even if not merged)
.TP
.BR \-\-kill\-port
Kill processes using the port
.TP
.BR \-\-kill\-\fICOMMAND\fR
Run Pwtfile COMMAND --kill before removal, then clear the allocated port
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-yes
Skip confirmation prompts
.RE
.TP
.B list\fR|\fBls \fR[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
List all worktrees with status information
.RS
.TP
.BR \-\-names
Output only worktree names (for scripting)
.TP
.BR \-\-json
Output as JSON
.RE
.TP
.B tree \fR[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
Visual tree view of worktrees
.RS
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-ports
Show port numbers
.RE

.SS Navigation
.TP
.B cd \fINAME\fR|\fIBRANCH\fR|\fI@\fR|\fI@there\fR|\fI-\fR|\fITERM\fR
Navigate to worktree. Resolves, in order: exact worktree name, exact
branch name (the worktree that has it checked out, main included), then
a search by name and description (case-insensitive).
If multiple matches found, opens fzf for selection.
Use \fB@\fR for main app, \fB-\fR for previous worktree.
.TP
.B @there
Go to the worktree of the branch named in the previous command line.
Made for git's "fatal: 'x' is already checked out at ...": the next
thing typed is \fBpwt @there\fR, nothing copied. Needs the shell
integration (\fBshell-init\fR), which hands the previous line over.
.TP
.B use \fINAME\fR|\fI@\fR
Set worktree as "current" (updates symlink). Supports partial name matching.
.TP
.B current \fR[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
Show current worktree
.RS
.TP
.BR \-\-resolved
Show resolved path instead of symlink
.RE
.TP
.B \-
Go to previous worktree

.SS Development
.TP
.B server\fR|\fBs \fR[\fINAME\fR] [\fB\-\-bg\fR [\fB\-\-progress json\fR]]
Start development server for worktree (runs Pwtfile server command). Every
boot is recorded as JSON lines in \fI~/.pwt/state/PROJECT/NAME.boot.jsonl\fR
(steps \fBboot\fR, \fBspawn\fR, \fBenvironment\fR, \fBlisten\fR, and any
\fBpwt_step\fR), and the last successful boot is kept as
\fINAME.boot.prev.jsonl\fR for wait estimates. With \fB\-\-bg \-\-progress
json\fR, stdout carries only those lines (human output goes to stderr) and
pwt exits 0 when the server listens, 1 when the boot fails, 5 on timeout
(router setting \fBboot_timeout\fR, default 120 seconds). Under
\fBmax_servers\fR, a \fB\-\-bg\fR start first stops the least recently
used idle server, or exits 4 when every running server is busy.
.TP
.B server wait \fR[\fINAME\fR] [\fB\-\-log\-contains\fR \fISTR\fR] [\fB\-\-timeout\fR \fISECONDS\fR]
Block until the worktree's server is ready: by default until its allocated
port accepts TCP connections, or until \fISTR\fR appears in the server job's
log. Exits 0 when ready, 1 when the port is held by a system daemon and so
can never belong to this worktree, 3 when there is no port or job, 5 on
timeout. Default timeout is 600 seconds.
.TP
.B jobs \fR[\fBlist\fR [\fB\-\-porcelain\fR]|\fBlogs\fR \fIID\fR|\fBwait\fR \fIID\fR|\fBstop\fR \fIID\fR|\fBclean\fR]
Manage background jobs started with \fB\-\-bg\fR. \fBlist \-\-porcelain\fR
emits JSON for scripts and Pwtfiles \(em prefer it over reading job files.
\fBwait\fR blocks until the job's process is gone (exit 5 on timeout).
\fBclean\fR removes stopped job records and their logs.
.TP
.B gateway up \fR[\fB\-\-port\fR \fIPORT\fR] [\fB\-\-host\fR \fIHOST\fR]
Start the project-scoped gateway proxy daemon. If a port is provided, it is
saved as the project's gateway_port. If a host is provided, it is saved as the
project's gateway_host and used in the public gateway URL.
.TP
.B gateway down
Stop the gateway proxy daemon for the current project. From outside a project,
use \fBpwt \fIPROJECT\fB gateway down\fR.
.TP
.B gateway use \fINAME\fR|\fB@\fR [\fB\-\-\fR \fISERVER_ARGS\fR...]
Route new gateway connections to the selected worktree's allocated server
port. If the target port is not listening and the project Pwtfile defines
server(), pwt starts it in the background first.
.TP
.B gateway status \fR[\fB\-\-json\fR]
Show gateway port, daemon status, and current target.
.TP
.B servers \fR[\fB\-\-all\fR] [\fB\-\-json\fR]
Show project server status, including gateway target, current worktree,
listening ports, and background server jobs. By default shows active entries;
\fB\-\-all\fR includes stopped worktrees. \fB\-\-json\fR rows carry the
public router port and URL, the last request seen by the router, requests in
flight, and a boot summary (state, duration, last good duration).
.TP
.B servers stop \fINAME\fR|\fB@\fR [\fB\-\-idle\fR]
Stop the pwt-managed (background) server jobs of a worktree. Exits 3 when pwt
runs no server for it (a foreground server is stopped where it runs). With
\fB\-\-idle\fR, refuses (exit 4) while a request or websocket is in flight
through the router, or while another pwt job runs for the worktree.
.TP
.B router up \fR[\fB\-\-port\fR \fIPORT\fR] [\fB\-\-listen\fR \fIADDRS\fR] [\fB\-\-foreground\fR]
Start the machine-wide router (node). Every worktree of every project gets a
stable public port, its pwt port plus \fBport_offset\fR (default 10000), that
the router owns while the server sleeps. The server keeps binding
\fB$PWT_PORT\fR and the router forwards to it, HTTP and WebSocket, with
X-Forwarded-For/Host/Proto/Port. A request to a stopped server runs
\fBpwt server NAME \-\-bg\fR: a browser gets a page with the boot steps
live and an estimate from the last boot, other clients are held up to
\fBhold_seconds\fR (default 30) and then get 503 with Retry-After. A failed
boot shows the error and the job log tail. \fB\-\-foreground\fR runs it
attached, for systemd or a container.
.TP
.B router down\fR|\fBrestart\fR|\fBstatus \fR[\fB\-\-json\fR]|\fBlogs \fR[\fB\-f\fR]
Stop, restart or inspect the router.
.TP
.B router routes \fR[\fB\-\-json\fR|\fB\-\-porcelain\fR]
List every public port and the worktree it serves.
.TP
.B router url \fR[\fINAME\fR|\fB@\fR]
Print a worktree's URL through the router.
.TP
.B router config \fR[\fIKEY\fR [\fIVALUE\fR|\fB\-\-unset\fR]]
Show or change router settings, stored as \fBrouter.KEY\fR in
\fI~/.pwt/config\fR: \fBport_offset\fR, \fBport\fR (status page, 7600),
\fBlisten\fR (127.0.0.1,::1), \fBidle_minutes\fR (30; 0 never; only servers
that got router traffic are stopped), \fBmax_servers\fR (0 = no cap; the
main app is never counted nor stopped), \fBboot_timeout\fR,
\fBhold_seconds\fR, \fBexpose_logs\fR, and the opt-in host routing keys
\fBhost_routing\fR, \fBdomain\fR, \fBtemplate\fR, \fBalias_template\fR,
\fBmain_template\fR, \fBmain_alias_template\fR, \fBscheme\fR,
\fBpublic_port\fR.
.TP
.B logs \fR[\fINAME\fR] [\fIJOB\fR] [\fB\-f\fR]
Show logs from a worktree's background jobs. Without a job id, picks the
server job (or the most recent job). \fB\-f\fR follows the log.
.TP
.B self \fR[\fBuse\fR \fITARGET\fR]
List installed pwt versions (aliases: \fBversions\fR, \fBwhich\fR). With
\fBuse\fR, point \fI~/.local/bin/pwt\fR at \fBlocal\fR, \fBnpm\fR,
\fBbrew\fR, a path, or a worktree of pwt's own repository.
.TP
.B run \fINAME\fR \fICOMMAND\fR...
Run command in specific worktree context
.TP
.B environment \fR[\fINAME\fR]
Print the shell assignments the Pwtfile's \fBenvironment()\fR gives the
worktree (default: current or main), for \fBeval "$(pwt environment)"\fR
or a tool that sources a file. pwt applies them itself before \fBrun\fR,
\fBfor\-each\fR and every Pwtfile command.
.TP
.B for\-each \fICOMMAND\fR...
Run command in all worktrees
.TP
.B editor \fR[\fINAME\fR]
Open worktree in configured editor

.SS Information
.TP
.B info \fINAME\fR
Show detailed worktree information, including branch, tracking target, port,
server status, directory, mode, description, created timestamp, and dirty files
.TP
.B port \fINAME\fR
Get allocated port for worktree
.TP
.B ports \fR[\fB\-\-json\fR]
Machine-wide port registry: every port pwt has allocated, in every project,
with the owning worktree, whether something is listening, and whether two
records claim the same port. Ports are allocated machine-wide, so a new
worktree never takes a port another project already owns. A port held by a
system daemon rather than a dev server is reported as \fBsystem\fR, not as
\fBlistening\fR: macOS AirPlay Receiver binds 5000 and 7000, which is where
most frameworks start.
.TP
.B diff \fIWORKTREE1\fR \fIWORKTREE2\fR
Show git diff between two worktrees
.TP
.B doctor
Check system health and configuration, including which pwt installation
actually answers on PATH (a shadowed install is the setup problem that
hides every other one)
.TP
.B skill \fR[\fB\-\-path\fR|\fB\-\-install\fR [\fIDIR\fR]]
Print the agent-facing guide to driving pwt (machine-readable flags, exit
codes, wait primitives, worktree lifecycle). \fB\-\-install\fR copies the
skill directory, default \fI~/.claude/skills/pwt\-cli\fR. For humans, see
\fBpwt help\fR.
.TP
.B state \fR[\fB\-\-json\fR]
Versioned snapshot of all pwt state (projects, worktrees with metadata,
background jobs). See \fBdocs/state\-v2\-contract.md\fR.
.TP
.B state migrate \fR[\fB\-\-status\fR|\fB\-\-check\fR|\fB\-\-verify\fR]
Inspect or apply state schema migrations. \fB\-\-check\fR is a read-only
dry run reporting what would convert and what would be skipped. Migration
also happens automatically on the first command after an upgrade.
See \fBdocs/migrations/\fR.

.SS Maintenance
.TP
.B repair\fR|\fBfix \fR[\fINAME\fR]
Repair broken worktree (runs Pwtfile repair command)
.TP
.B auto\-remove \fR[\fITARGET\fR]
Remove worktrees whose branches are merged into target
.TP
.B fix\-port \fINAME\fR
Resolve port conflict by allocating new port
.TP
.B restore \fINAME\fR
Recover backed up changes from trash

.SS Patches
.TP
.B patch apply \fR[\fINAME\fR] \fIFILE\fR|\fB\-\fR [\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-\-repair\fR] [\fB\-\-idle\fR] [\fB\-\-detached\fR] [\fB\-\-strip\-session\fR] [\fB\-\-allow\-secrets\fR] [\fB\-p\fIN\fR]
Land the commits of a git format-patch mailbox on the worktree with
\fBgit am \-3\fR (authors kept), or apply a plain diff to its files with no
commit. Without \fINAME\fR, the worktree you stand in. Commits the branch
already has (same patch-id) are skipped. Refuses (exit 4) with nothing
changed when a touched file has local edits, when the index has staged
changes, while an am, rebase, merge, cherry-pick, revert or bisect is in
progress, on a detached HEAD (\fB\-\-detached\fR overrides), when a message
carries a session trailer or link (\fB\-\-strip\-session\fR drops them), when
the patch touches a secret path such as \fI.env\fR or \fI*.pem\fR
(\fB\-\-allow\-secrets\fR), and, with \fB\-\-idle\fR, while a pwt job runs for
the worktree. It never stashes. The HEAD before the apply and a patch of any
local edits go to \fI~/.pwt/trash\fR first (\fBpwt restore\fR lists them).
On a conflict the apply is aborted (exit 1), the worktree is left as it was
and the conflicting files are named. Afterwards the Pwtfile's
\fBrepair()\fR sees \fB$PWT_CHANGED_FILES\fR and \fB$PWT_PREVIOUS_COMMIT\fR;
it is suggested, and run only with \fB\-\-repair\fR. \fB\-\-json\fR prints one
object (\fIresult\fR, \fIreason\fR, \fIhead_before\fR, \fIhead_after\fR,
\fIapplied\fR, \fIskipped\fR, \fItouched_files\fR, \fIthree_way_files\fR,
\fIconflict_files\fR, \fIbackup\fR, \fIundo\fR). Exits 3 for an unknown
worktree or file, 2 for an empty or unreadable input.
.TP
.B patch export \fR[\fINAME\fR] [\fB\-\-base \fIREF\fR] [\fB\-o \fIFILE\fR] [\fB\-\-json\fR]
Write the worktree's own commits as a mailbox (\fBgit format-patch
\-\-binary\fR) to stdout or \fIFILE\fR. The range starts at the merge base
with the worktree's recorded base branch, or at \fIREF\fR. The commit count
goes to stderr, with a warning when the range has merge commits.
\fB\-\-json\fR needs \fB\-o\fR.

.SS Interactive Selection
.TP
.B select \fR[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
Interactive worktree selector with preview, keybindings, and query support.
Uses \fBfzf\fR when installed; otherwise falls back to a builtin picker.
.RS
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-query " " \fITEXT\fR
Pre-fill search query
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-dirty
Only show dirty worktrees
.RE

.SS Metadata
.TP
.B meta\fR|\fBm \fIKEY\fR
Get metadata field from the worktree you are standing in (shortcut; falls
back to the current-symlink worktree when outside any worktree).
.TP
.B meta\fR|\fBm \fIKEY\fR \fIVALUE\fR
Set metadata field on the worktree you are standing in (shortcut; same
resolution as the get form).
.TP
.B meta \fI"text with spaces"\fR
Set description on current worktree (space heuristic).
.TP
.B meta list
List all metadata
.TP
.B meta show \fINAME\fR
Show metadata for worktree
.TP
.B meta set \fINAME\fR \fIFIELD\fR \fIVALUE\fR
Update metadata field
.TP
.B meta unset \fR[\fINAME\fR] \fIFIELD\fR
Remove a custom metadata field (phase, reviewer, marker, ...). Without
\fINAME\fR, the worktree is resolved from the current directory. Structural
fields (path, branch, port, base, base_commit, created_at) cannot be unset.

.SS Steps (Pwtfile Automation)
.TP
.B steps
List available step functions from the project Pwtfile.
.TP
.B step \fINAME\fR [\fIARGS\fR]
Run a single step function (defined as step_NAME() in Pwtfile).

.SS Project Alias
.TP
.B alias \fR[\fINAME\fR|\fB\-\-clear\fR]
Set a short alias for the current project. Without arguments, shows current alias.

.SS Plugins
.TP
.B plugin list
List installed plugins
.TP
.B plugin install \fISOURCE\fR
Install plugin from file or URL
.TP
.B plugin remove \fINAME\fR
Remove plugin
.TP
.B plugin create \fINAME\fR
Create new plugin template

.SH PWTFILE
A Pwtfile is a bash script in your project root that defines custom commands. Available functions:

The pwt core stays project-agnostic: it manages worktrees, metadata, ports,
navigation, jobs, and delegation. Project-specific dependencies, databases,
workers, tests, and cleanup belong in Pwtfile commands.

.TP
.B server()
Start development server. Receives $PWT_PORT, $PWT_WORKTREE, etc.
.TP
.B repair()
Fix worktree issues (reinstall deps, reset state)
.TP
.B setup()
Run after worktree creation
.TP
.B cleanup()
Run before worktree removal
.TP
.B environment()
Print shell assignments for the worktree it runs in (for example
\fBmise env \-s bash\fR or \fBdirenv export bash\fR). A version manager
that switches on a shell hook never fires in the subshell pwt runs commands
in; pwt applies these assignments before \fBpwt run\fR, \fBfor\-each\fR and
every Pwtfile command, and prints them with \fBpwt environment\fR. The global
Pwtfile's come first and the project's last, so the project wins. A failing
\fBenvironment()\fR warns and the command runs without it.
.TP
.B repoint()
Run by \fBpwt repoint\fR after the checkout. \fB$PWT_CHANGED_FILES\fR lists
the changed paths, one per line, so it can redo only the steps whose inputs
changed (\fBpwt_changed\fR).
.TP
.B hosts()
Extra hostnames for the router's opt-in host routing, one per line
(\fBapi\fR), or \fB*\fR to route every label in front of the worktree's own
host to it.
.TP
.I custom_command()
Any function becomes available as \fBpwt custom_command\fR

.SS Helper Functions
Available inside Pwtfile functions:
.TP
.B pwtfile_copy \fIpath\fR
Copy a file or directory from the main checkout into the worktree
.TP
.B pwtfile_symlink \fIpath\fR
Symlink a path from the main checkout into the worktree
.TP
.B pwtfile_replace_literal \fIfile old new\fR / \fBpwtfile_replace_re\fR \fIfile pattern replacement\fR
Rewrite configuration values inside the worktree
.TP
.B pwtfile_git_exclude \fIpattern\fR...
Append patterns to the repo's \fB.git/info/exclude\fR (common dir): ignored
like .gitignore entries but never committed, and one call covers every
worktree of the repo. Idempotent per pattern.
.TP
.B run \fIcmd\fR...
Run a command, silently tolerating failure
.TP
.B pwt_step \fIname\fR \fIcmd\fR... / \fBpwt_step\fR \fIname\fR \fB\-\-skip\fR [\fIreason\fR]
Time one step of a server boot (or a repoint): writes start, done or fail
lines with the duration and returns the command's status. Outside a boot it
just runs the command.
.TP
.B pwt_changed \fIglob\fR...
In \fBrepoint()\fR: succeed when a changed file matches a glob (bash
patterns, \fB*\fR also crosses \fB/\fR).

.SS Command Forms
.TP
.B pwt \fIPROJECT WORKTREE COMMAND\fR [args...]
Run a Pwtfile command for a worktree from anywhere.
.TP
.B pwt \fIWORKTREE COMMAND\fR [args...]
Run a Pwtfile command for a worktree from inside the project.
.TP
.B pwt \fICOMMAND\fR [args...]
Run a Pwtfile command from inside the current worktree.
.PP
Command arguments are passed as positional shell arguments (\fB$1\fR,
\fB$2\fR, ...) and as raw \fB$PWT_ARGS\fR.

.SS Available Variables
.TP
.B $PWT_PORT
Allocated port number
.TP
.B $PWT_WORKTREE
Worktree name
.TP
.B $PWT_WORKTREE_PATH
Full path to worktree
.TP
.B $PWT_BRANCH
Git branch name
.TP
.B $PWT_PROJECT
Project name
.TP
.B $PWT_ARGS
Arguments passed to command
.TP
.B $PWT_KILL_TARGET
Delegated command name from \fBremove --kill-\fICOMMAND\fR
.TP
.B $PWT_PUBLIC_PORT\fR, \fB$PWT_PUBLIC_URL
The stable port the router serves this worktree on, and
http://localhost:PORT for it
.TP
.B $PWT_BOOT_FILE
Progress file of the server boot in progress (empty otherwise)
.TP
.B $PWT_CHANGED_FILES\fR, \fB$PWT_PREVIOUS_COMMIT
In \fBrepoint()\fR: the changed paths and the commit the worktree was on
.TP
.B $PWT_HOST\fR, \fB$PWT_URL\fR, \fB$PWT_HOSTS\fR, \fB$PWT_DOMAIN\fR, \fB$PWT_HOST_\fIALIAS
Router host names; filled only with \fBrouter.host_routing\fR true

.SH CONFIGURATION
Configuration is stored in \fB~/.pwt/\fR:

.TP
.B ~/.pwt/projects/\fINAME\fR/config.json
Project configuration (path, worktrees_dir, branch_prefix, base_port, gateway_port, gateway_host)
.TP
.B ~/.pwt/projects/\fINAME\fR/metadata.json
Worktree metadata (ports, branches, descriptions)
.TP
.B ~/.pwt/plugins/
Custom plugins

.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Initialize a project:
.B pwt init
.TP
Clone and initialize:
.B pwt init git@github.com:user/repo.git
.TP
Create worktree from current branch:
.B pwt create feature-auth --from-current
.TP
Track an existing remote branch:
.B pwt track origin/team/PROJ-123
.TP
Create named worktree with exact branch:
.B pwt create PROJ-123 --branch team/PROJ-123 --from origin/team/PROJ-123
.TP
Create worktree with description:
.B pwt create PROJ-123 main "Add user authentication"
.TP
Start server:
.B pwt server my-feature
.TP
Run command in all worktrees:
.B pwt for-each ./scripts/test
.TP
Remove merged worktrees:
.B pwt auto-remove main
.TP
Use project prefix from anywhere:
.B pwt myproject list

.SH SHELL INTEGRATION
Add to your shell config:

.SS Zsh (~/.zshrc)
.nf
eval "$(pwt shell-init zsh)"
.fi

.SS Bash (~/.bashrc)
.nf
eval "$(pwt shell-init bash)"
.fi

.SS Fish (~/.config/fish/config.fish)
.nf
pwt shell-init fish | source
.fi

This enables \fBpwt cd\fR integration for actual directory change.

.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B PWT_DEBUG
Enable debug output when set to 1
.TP
.B PWT_NO_PERL
Set to 1 to detach background processes without perl (a subshell that
closes inherited descriptors and calls \fBsetsid\fR(1) when available),
the path minimal Linux images take anyway.
.TP
.B PWT_NO_UPDATE_CHECK
Set to 1 to disable the update notice and the background version check
entirely. The notice is shown at most once a day, on stderr, only for an
interactive terminal, and never under \fB--no-input\fR or for agents.
.TP
.B EDITOR
Editor for \fBpwt editor\fR command

.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.pwt/
Main configuration directory
.TP
.I ~/.pwt/projects/
Project configurations
.TP
.I ~/.pwt/plugins/
Custom plugins
.TP
.I ~/.pwt/trash/
Backup of removed worktrees
.TP
.I ~/.pwt/router/
Router pid, log, readiness marker and per-worktree activity
.TP
.I ~/.pwt/state/PROJECT/NAME.boot.jsonl
Progress of the last server boot (\fI.boot.prev.jsonl\fR: last good one)
.TP
.I ~/.pwt/version-check
Cached latest released version (refreshed in the background)
.TP
.I Pwtfile
Project-level command definitions

.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success
.TP
.B 1
General error (a \fBpatch apply\fR aborted on conflict is this)
.TP
.B 2
Usage or argument error
.TP
.B 3
Not found (worktree, project, branch, port, job)
.TP
.B 4
Conflict (already exists, port in use, busy worktree, a refusal before any change)
.TP
.B 5
Timeout (\fBjobs wait\fR, \fBserver wait\fR, a \fB\-\-progress json\fR boot)
.TP
.B 6
Missing dependency (a required external tool is not installed)

